In this lesson we'll look at two more curve families you should be able to recognise on sight. First, circles centered at the origin. When you see the square of x plus the square of y equal to some radius squared, picture a perfectly round circle sitting on the origin, with that radius being the distance from the middle out to the edge. No asymptotes, and the domain and range just depend on how big the radius is. Second, exponentials of the form y equals a times b to the x. Think of a as where the curve crosses the y-axis, and b as what controls its behaviour. If b is bigger than one, you get growth — the curve shoots upward quickly. If b is between zero and one, you get decay — it falls toward the x-axis. In both cases the curve lives above the x-axis and never touches it, so the x-axis is your horizontal asymptote. Now let's see it in action.